  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The upper level low centered off the \nCalifornia coast continues to push moisture into the Great Basin \neven as it meanders east. Current radar imagery shows the majority\nof the showers and thunderstorms in Utah and Arizona, however \ndevelopment closer to home is expected to begin within the next \nhour. Thunderstorms continue through tomorrow night and a few may \neven become severe, with a marginal risk of severe thunderstorms \ncovering White Pine, southeastern Elko and northeastern Nye \nCounties for tomorrow. Showers will continue through Friday \nevening, particularly in eastern Nevada, and some high elevation \nsnow mixes in beginning Thursday night as snow levels drop from \naround 10,000 feet to around 7,000 feet. Valley snow accumulation \nis not expected. Road accumulation is also not expected, with the \nexception of Mountain City Highway near the Idaho border, which \ncould see about half an inch Thursday night into Friday. Total \nliquid equivalent accumulation through Friday evening is between a\nthird and three quarters of an inch, with some mountain tops \nreaching one inch.\n\nThe upper low center is expected to be over northwest Arizona by\nFriday night, putting Northeastern Nevada under a cold and dry\nnortheast flow. Friday night is the coldest night of the forecast,\nwith lows in the 20s across the area. Temperatures rebound\nsomewhat as the low exits the Southwest but they remain slightly\nbelow seasonal, with highs in the upper 50s to low 60s and lows in\nthe upper 20s to low 30s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in showers and\nembedded thunderstorms through tomorrow night. Moderate confidence\nin high elevation snow Thursday night and Friday. High confidence\nin near seasonal temperatures beyond Friday. PoPs were increased\nand expanded to better reflect the incoming precipitation event.",
